If it helps frame the options while you fetch it, engineers who build monitoring around cache “workarounds” often hold one of these mental models:
- Cache as necessary safety valve: upstream is unreliable/slow, so warmers and manual flushes are “by design.”
- Eventual consistency as a norm: transient staleness is expected; alerts watch refill times and miss rates rather than root causes.
- Throughput protection: caches protect databases; eviction/warming rituals are part of capacity control, not suspected bugs.
- Irreducible flakiness: certain spikes/poisoned keys “always happen,” so monitors detect patterns to react fast instead of questioning them.

